# The services here run longer than their check interval. The reference
# starts such a check again while earlier runs of it are still going, and
# takes each run's result as it comes. checkd does not run two checks of
# one object at once: the next check is planned when the result is in.
# So the services are checked at other moments, service s7 gets to its
# hard state later, and host a, which is checked whenever one of its
# services turns bad, is checked at other moments too.
#
# What is left to compare is the hosts whose own check is slow. The
# reference does not start a host check again while one is going.
ignore s3
ignore s5
ignore s7
ignore q
ignore a
ignore-log a;s7

# A contact's line about a notification it was not sent is written once
# for a reason, whichever service it is about. Which of these services
# comes first depends on when they are checked, which differs here.
ignore-log a;s3
ignore-log a;s5
